#!/bin/bash
# wg-peer/help.loc - Module-specific help

cat <<'EOF'
Module: wg-peer - spoke side of a WireGuard network

  Configures a machine that dials OUT to a hub, holds the tunnel open through
  NAT, and re-resolves the hub's address when it changes.

  Generic. Nothing here is backup-specific. WHY a given network is configured
  the way it is lives in BACKUP.md.

SUBCOMMANDS

  setup                  Install, generate a keypair, write the interface,
                         install the re-resolve timer, print the peer line.
      yamc -h BOX -e net=bkup -e addr=10.9.9.2 wg-peer
      yamc -h BOX -e net=bkup -e addr=10.9.9.2 -e yes=1 wg-peer

  status                 Interface, handshake age, DDNS drift, timer. Read-only.
      yamc -h BOX -e net=bkup wg-peer status

  probe                  Actively prove the box cannot reach past the hub.
                         Temporarily widens its own config, then reverts.
      yamc -h BOX -e net=bkup wg-peer probe

  key                    Show this peer's line for <net>.peers; -e save=1
                         appends it. -e force=1 replaces a line whose key
                         changed (i.e. the box was rebuilt).
      yamc -h BOX -e net=bkup wg-peer key
      yamc -h BOX -e net=bkup -e save=1 wg-peer key

  -e net=NAME  REQUIRED, no default.
  -e addr=ADDR REQUIRED the first time, no default. Without it (and without a
               line for this host in <net>.peers) the module lists the
               addresses in use and stops - auto-picking risks a collision,
               and WireGuard does not error on overlapping AllowedIPs, it
               silently reassigns and misroutes the loser. Later runs read it
               from this host's line in <net>.peers.

KEYS

  The private key is generated ON THIS MACHINE and never leaves it - not to
  yamc, not into yamc.local. Only the public key comes back: a successful
  setup run appends this host's line to yamc.local/wgnet/<net>.peers
  (never replacing a different one). Then deploy the table to the hub:
      yamc -h HUB -e net=NAME -e yes=1 wg-hub

THE TWO /32s

  Address    = <this box>/32
  AllowedIPs = <hub>/32          the hub, and nothing else

  So this machine's routing table contains no route toward the hub's LAN at
  all. Combined with the matching /32 in the hub's peer stanza, isolation is
  structural at both ends rather than a firewall rule either side could get
  wrong.

  AllowedIPs = 0.0.0.0/0 would instead route ALL of this machine's traffic
  through the tunnel - correct for a laptop on hotel wifi, wrong for a remote
  box that should only be able to reach the hub.

ENDPOINT RE-RESOLUTION

  wg-quick resolves the endpoint hostname ONCE, at interface start. When a
  dynamic-DNS name changes address, the tunnel stays down until something
  restarts it. On an unattended box at a remote site that is the failure that
  matters most, and it is silent.

  So a systemd timer runs every 2 minutes and re-resolves ONLY when the
  handshake has actually gone stale (>150s), leaving a working tunnel alone:

    wg set bk0 peer <hub pubkey> endpoint bateman.cc:51822

  PersistentKeepalive=25 handles the other half - it holds the NAT mapping at
  the remote site open, and lets the hub learn this peer's current address.

  'wg-peer status' compares what the endpoint resolves to NOW against what the
  running tunnel is using, so DDNS drift is visible rather than mysterious.

ISOLATION PROBE  ('wg-peer probe')

  Proves this box cannot reach past the hub. Targets come from <net>.conf:

    WG_LAN_PROBE      a LAN host beyond the hub  -> tests the FORWARD chain
    WG_HUB_LAN_ADDR   the hub's own LAN address  -> tests the INPUT chain

  Both must report "blocked (correct)". They exercise different paths and a
  config can pass one while failing the other - which is exactly what happened
  here: a forward-only policy left every service on the hub's LAN address
  reachable.

  If the network sets WG_HUB_PORTS, a third 'port' check connects to the
  hub's tunnel address on a port outside the list (22 unless listed). It must
  time out; a refusal also fails, because a RST means the packet got through.

  WHY IT IS ACTIVE. Merely trying to reach a LAN address proves nothing:
  routing sends only the hub /32 through the tunnel, so the attempt never
  touches the tunnel. It succeeds on your own LAN for the wrong reason, and
  fails at a remote site for the wrong reason - false reassurance being the
  dangerous one. So the probe does what a rooted box would do: adds a /32
  route via the tunnel and widens its own AllowedIPs, attempts the
  connection, and puts both back. The revert is trapped, so it happens even
  on error or interrupt.

  RUN IT ON THE BENCH. A /32 route via the tunnel outranks the connected LAN
  route, so the test is valid while the box is still on your own network -
  and that is when a failure is cheapest to fix.

      yamc -h BOX -e net=bkup wg-peer probe

MANUAL EQUIVALENT

    apt-get install wireguard-tools
    umask 077
    wg genkey | tee /etc/wireguard/bk0.key | wg pubkey > /etc/wireguard/bk0.pub

    cat > /etc/wireguard/bk0.conf <<'END'
    [Interface]
    Address    = 10.9.9.2/32
    PrivateKey = <contents of bk0.key>

    [Peer]
    PublicKey           = <hub public key>
    Endpoint            = bateman.cc:51822
    AllowedIPs          = 10.9.9.1/32
    PersistentKeepalive = 25
    END

    systemctl enable --now wg-quick@bk0
    cat /etc/wireguard/bk0.pub        # hand this to the hub operator

  Check:  wg show bk0     ('latest handshake' should be under ~2 minutes)
          ping 10.9.9.1   (the hub, and nothing beyond it)

INTERFACE NAMING

  Do not name it wg*. See 'wg-hub help' - the wg+ wildcard in common firewall
  snippets is the reason, and it applies to both ends. The module refuses.

SEE ALSO

  wg-hub                 the hub side
  BACKUP.md              system design and threat model
EOF
